.TH GIT-NESTED 1 "2026-09-06" "git-nested" "Git Nested Manual"
.SH NAME
git-nested \- keep another repository inside a subdirectory of your own
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git nested
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git-nested
puts the contents of another repository into a subdirectory of yours and
commits them as ordinary files. Anyone who clones your repository gets those
files with it. There is nothing extra to initialise, no second set of access
rights to hand out, and every commit of yours is complete on its own.
.PP
.B git nested clone
also works in a new repository with no commits yet: the clone commit becomes
the root commit there.
.PP
Each such subdirectory holds a
.B .gitnested
file recording where the content came from and how far it has been kept up to
date. That file is what
.B git nested pull
and
.B git nested push
use to move changes between your subdirectory and its upstream.
.PP
The commands that read only committed nested history \-\-
.BR branch ,
.B diff
and
.B "push without --commit"
\-\- do not need a clean parent working tree.
If the nested subdirectory itself has uncommitted tracked changes, git-nested warns and ignores them.
.PP
If a tracked directory already holds upstream content, either
.B git nested clone --adopt
or
.BI "git nested init -r " url " --commit " sha
can connect it to that upstream without replacing its files.

.BR "git nested clone" ,
.B git nested pull
and
.B git nested push
accept
.B \-\-rebase
and
.B \-\-merge
as short forms of
.B "\-M rebase"
and
.BR "\-M merge" .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B branch
create a branch holding the local nested commits
.TP
.B clean
remove the branches, refs and remotes nested operations created
.TP
.B clone
clone an upstream repository into a subdirectory
.TP
.B commit
add a nested branch to the current history as one commit
.TP
.B completion
print the shell completion script for git-nested
.TP
.B config
read or write a nested repository's configuration
.TP
.B diff
show a nested repository's local changes against upstream
.TP
.B fetch
fetch upstream content for a nested repository
.TP
.B init
turn an existing subdirectory into a nested repository
.TP
.B pull
update a nested repository from its upstream
.TP
.B push
push a nested repository's local changes upstream
.TP
.B repair
point a nested repository's sync point back into the current history
.TP
.B run
run a git command in the worktree of a nested repository
.TP
.B status
report on the nested repositories of this repository
.TP
.B version
print version information
.PP
Run
.BI "git nested " command " --help"
for what a command does, its options and some examples.
.SH OPTIONS
These are accepted before any command.
.TP
.B \-\-version
print the git-nested version number
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
report only warnings and errors
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
narrate the steps being taken; twice to log every git command
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <subdir>/.gitnested
YAML, one per nested subdirectory, committed to your repository and never
pushed upstream. It records the upstream remote and branch, the last upstream
commit taken, the commit of yours the two were last equal at, and whether
pulls merge or rebase and whether they squash. Read and write it with
.BR "git nested config" .
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Take a library into
.IR ext/lib :
.PP
.RS
.nf
git nested clone https://github.com/user/lib ext/lib
git nested clone --adopt https://github.com/user/lib ext/lib

.br
git nested clone --rebase https://github.com/user/lib ext/lib
.fi
.RE
.PP
Bring in what upstream has done since:
.PP
.RS
.nf
git nested pull ext/lib
.br
git nested pull --no-squash ext/lib

git nested pull --rebase ext/lib

git nested pull --all -b release/2.0
.fi
.RE
.PP
Send your changes back:
.PP
.RS
.nf
git nested push ext/lib
.br
git nested push --rebase ext/lib

git nested push --all -b release/2.0
.fi
.RE
.PP
Fetch one shared branch across all nested repositories:
.PP
.RS
.nf
git nested fetch --all -b release/2.0
.fi
.RE
.PP
See where every nested subdirectory stands:
.PP
.RS
.nf
git nested status
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success
.TP
.B 1
the command failed; the reason is on standard error
.TP
.B 130
interrupted
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR git-submodule (1),
.BR git-subtree (1)
.PP
